HI all, I have some unsampled cells that I would like to estimate occupancy for.  I need estimates for 3 year consecutive years (the same years I have in my sampled population).  I;m using the colext function and find a "best" model.  There are no covariates in the occupancy equation that change over time (but there are covariates in colonization and extinction rates that are annual). If I create a new data file and run projected(best, type="psi", new.data), I get the same occupancy rate for each year (because  the covariates are the same). alternatively I could use the recursive equation as described in MacKenzie et al. to calculate occupancy for the unsurveyed sites.

Note that from ﬁrst year occupancy (psi1), colonization (g(t)) and extinction probabilities (e(t)),
occupancy in future years (yt+1) can be calculated with the recursive equation:
y(t+1) = y(t)*(1-epsilon (t) + (1-y(t))*g(t)

Then I would get different answers for each year.

So I guess what I'm asking is whether the unconditional occupancy rates estimated for years 2 and years 3 (for example best@projected) are based on the above equation or not?  In other words is the intercept value for occupancy from the "best" model, the expected occupancy in year 1 and then year 2 and year 3 are estimated from the extinction colonization rates?

Thanks,

Robin

